#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Run the fast test suites before anything leaves this machine.
#
# --fast, not the full run, and the reasoning matters because it is a real
# trade-off rather than an oversight.
#
# The full run is 18 suites, and the 9 suites that drive a real terminal, editor
# or language server are where nearly all of the time goes — minutes rather than
# seconds. A pre-push hook that takes minutes is one you start passing
# --no-verify to, and a hook that is routinely bypassed is worse than no hook at
# all, because it still looks like coverage.
#
# THERE IS NO TIMING IN THIS HEADER, and that is a rule rather than an omission.
# The one that used to be here was wrong by more than an order of magnitude by
# the time anyone read it, and the paragraph beneath it then argued from a
# second number that had gone stale the same way. A timing is true of one
# machine on one day; tests/test-performance.sh times this hook's suite where it
# actually runs and fails it against a ceiling, which is the only place such a
# number can stay true. checks/repo.sh rejects any figure in this file that it
# cannot read back out of the repo — the two counts above it reads out of tests/.
#
# What --fast skips is not unguarded: CI runs the COMPLETE suite on every push,
# so the pty-driven suites are checked on the way in rather than on the way out.
# The window this leaves open is a regression that reaches the remote and is
# caught minutes later by CI instead of instantly here. That is the price, and
# it buys a hook that actually runs.
#
# Bypass with: git push --no-verify
exec "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/tests/run.sh" --fast
